"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"parents heterozygous carriers","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"Globa et al. 2022","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["Patient 3 was previously briefly mentioned in the report discussing possible cases of sex development disorders in Ukrainian descendants (Globa et al. 2022). The patient is an 18-year-old man of Ukrainian descent with no significant family history.","The patient underwent a gonadectomy of the first testicle at the age of 10 and the second one at the age of 15; the histological report confirmed atrophic and sclerotic changes in the testicles and no signs of spermatogenesis.","The patient was diagnosed with intellectual disability. A WES study (at 14) identified a homozygous variant c.100 + 1G > A in the TBCE."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:39657131:1","pmid":"39657131","label":"7 patients with novel milder TBCE phenotype","kind":"group","family":"","n_affected":7,"n_families":null,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"amyotrophy","hpo_label":"Skeletal muscle atrophy","hpo_id":"HP:0003202","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"testicular failure","hpo_label":"Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","hpo_id":"HP:0000815","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"mild intellectual disability","hpo_label":"Mild intellectual disability","hpo_id":"HP:0001256","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"short stature","hpo_label":"Short stature","hpo_id":"HP:0004322","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"reduced Golgi compactness in dermal fibroblasts","hpo_label":"Abnormal cellular phenotype","hpo_id":"HP:0025354","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"acto-myosin degradation on electron microscopy","hpo_label":"Abnormal muscle fiber morphology","hpo_id":"HP:0004303","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"TBCE","as_reported":"NM_003193.5:c.100 + 1G > A","cdna":"c.100+1G>A","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"unknown","origin":"unknown","consequence":"splice site","key":"V1"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["We report 7 patients with a novel, milder TBCE phenotype, presenting with amyotrophy, testicular failure, and mild intellectual disability, with or without short stature.","All patients were homozygous or compound-heterozygous for the NM_003193.5:c.100 + 1G > A variant, which causes a splicing alteration and early frameshift.","Dermal fibroblasts showed reduced Golgi compactness but normal microtubule dynamics. Electron microscopy revealed varying levels of acto-myosin degradation."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:39657131:1","pmid":"39657131","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Sparber et al. (2025) found that the TBCE c.100+1G>A variant caused a splicing change and an early frameshift. However, translation from an alternative open reading frame produced a partially functional protein, which explained the milder phenotype.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["All patients were homozygous or compound-heterozygous for the NM_003193.5:c.100 + 1G > A variant, which causes a splicing alteration and early frameshift.","However, we found that the mild phenotype arises due to translation from an alternative open reading frame, producing a partially functional protein."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:39657131:2","pmid":"39657131","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Sparber et al. (2025) found that patient dermal fibroblasts had a less compact Golgi complex but normal microtubule dynamics after nocodazole treatment. Electron microscopy showed varying degrees of acto-myosin degradation.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Dermal fibroblasts showed reduced Golgi compactness but normal microtubule dynamics. Electron microscopy revealed varying levels of acto-myosin degradation."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:39657131:3","pmid":"39657131","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Sparber et al. (2025) found that the TBCE c.100+1G>A variant was 10 times more frequent in Slavic samples than in gnomAD, which suggested that this phenotype is underdiagnosed.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The c.100 + 1G > A variant was found to be 10 times more frequent in Slavic samples than in gnomAD, suggesting underdiagnosis of this phenotype."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:39153170:1","pmid":"39153170","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Badura-Stronka et al. (2025) identified a homozygous TBCE splice-donor variant in intron 2 (c.100+1G>A) by whole-exome sequencing in 3 unrelated patients, 2 Polish and 1 Ukrainian. Each patient's parents were heterozygous carriers. ClinVar listed conflicting classifications for the variant.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["A homozygous pathogenic variant c.100 + 1G > A in TBCE was detected in three patients (P1-P3), followed by the confirmation of heterozygous carrier status of the variant in their parents.","In the c.100 + 1G > A variant, the sequence alteration impacts a donor splice site within intron 2 of the TBCE, potentially disrupting RNA splicing."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:39153170:2","pmid":"39153170","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Badura-Stronka et al. (2025) compared these patients with previously reported TBCE patients. They found that c.100+1G>A homozygotes had a milder course than patients with the c.155_166del or c.464T>A variants. The c.100+1G>A homozygotes had later onset (mean age 5 years), normal motor development and eventual normal speech. They also had normal calcium-phosphate metabolism, no significant brain MRI changes, and cryptorchidism in males.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Homozygous carriers of the c.100 + 1G > A variant exhibit a markedly milder clinical course, with no deviations in the calcium-phosphate metabolism and central nervous system pathology in MRI studies.","The primary clinical differences compared to homozygous c.464 T > A individuals include the onset of symptoms at a later developmental stage, normal motor development, eventual proper speech development, and cryptorchidism in boys."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:39153170:3","pmid":"39153170","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Badura-Stronka et al. (2025) noted that in the c.100+1G>A patients, muscle atrophy mainly affected proximal muscles. Increased tone was most marked in the paraspinal muscles, producing a rigid spine, a feature not previously documented in TBCE-related conditions.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["In patients P1 and P2, an additional symptom was a rigid spine, a trait not previously documented in TBCE-related conditions.","Muscle atrophy primarily affects proximal muscles, and increased muscle tone is most pronounced in the paraspinal muscles, leading to spinal mobility limitations."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:39153170:4","pmid":"39153170","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Badura-Stronka et al. (2025) noted that the c.100+1G>A TBCE variant had so far been found only in patients of Central-Eastern European descent, which suggests a possible founder mutation in this population.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The identification of the pathogenic c.100 + 1G > A variant has thus far been limited to patients of Central-Eastern European descent, suggesting a potential founder mutation in this population."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["TBCE"],"confirmed":["TBCE"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}